   "numbers": [
    {
     "text": "The 1981 Appalachian land study (80 counties, six states, ~20 million acres, ~55,000 parcels, researched by about 100 local residents): **about 75% of surveyed surface acres and 80% of mineral rights were absentee-owned;** corporations held 40% of the land and **70% of mineral rights.**",
     "src": "*Who Owns Appalachia?* via *Facing South*, Jan 1982",
     "mark": "care",
     "note": "Six-state region, 45 years old."
    },
    {
     "text": "**More than 75% of mineral owners paid less than 25 cents an acre in property tax.**",
     "src": "the 1981 study via *YES!*, 2017",
     "mark": "care",
     "note": "Regional, old."
    },
    {
     "text": "The study found ownership highly concentrated among a few absentee and corporate owners.",
     "src": "via *YES!*",
     "mark": "ok",
     "note": ""
    },
    {
     "text": "In the four hardest-hit 2022 flood counties, mountainous land limits buildable parcels and raises the cost of building.",
     "src": "Cleveland Fed",
     "mark": "ok",
     "note": ""
    }
   ],
   "plain": "In much of eastern Kentucky, the people who live on the land do not own what is under it. A century ago, mineral rights were bought cheaply and separated from the surface; most of those rights, and much of the land itself, came to be owned by companies and people far away, and were taxed lightly. That decided what a county could collect for schools and roads, and what a family could do with its own ground.",
   "chain": [],
   "contested": "Who owns the minerals and land in Floyd County *today* — ownership has shifted with bankruptcies, land companies and conservation buyers.",
   "who": "Written down, with dates: the deeds, the court rulings, and the tax rules. Mineral owners and their shareholders, mostly outside the region, gained the coal at low tax. Counties and their schools paid in lost revenue; surface owners paid in damaged land and wells. Some of this was later changed by people here (below).",
   "nlb": "Nature: coal under the mountains. Luck: whether your family's land had a seam worth buying. **Built:** the deeds, the courts' reading of them, the tax rates. *Built, and written down.*",
   "doing": "**The land study itself** — about 100 local residents researched who owned their own counties. ✅ It led to the **Kentucky Fair Tax Coalition**, now **Kentuckians For The Commonwealth (KFTC)**, which helped end strip mining over surface owners' objections. ✅ (*YES!*). That is not resilience; it is a research project and a statewide organizing campaign by people who were told it couldn't be changed.",
   "denver": "(D-WATER is lead service lines, a different structure.) Ask for a new key before publishing this line. Colorado's coal history — the southern coalfield company towns and the 1914 Ludlow Massacre — is the same story of who owned the ground and who worked it. ⚠️ (History recalled; source before publishing.)",
