 "cushionNotice": "No single row is the story. Stack them: an older Floyd County homeowner meets a fall, a flood or a death in the family with **public cushions** — Medicaid, Social Security, SNAP — and almost no private ones: no flood insurance, no paid leave, no equity to borrow against, a minimum wage less than half of Denver's. Many RJ families meet the same events with private cushions they did not build and rarely see — an employer's health plan, a mortgage-backed home that gains value, a 401(k), FAMLI, a credit line. **Here the cushion is visible because it comes with a form to fill out. At home it is invisible because it comes with a job.** That is the fence (2b).",
 "fences": [
  {
   "h": "Insulation",
   "text": "Our families' cushions come through jobs and property, so we don't see them as help. A Kentucky family's cushions come through Medicaid and Social Security, so we do — and we judge. See 2c."
  },
  {
   "h": "Physical",
   "text": "Eight days, a retreat center in an old schoolhouse, work sites 40 minutes away, rental cars. We see houses mostly from the outside and from the porch we are building. We meet the people who need a ramp; we don't meet the people who are doing fine, and we can come away thinking everyone needs one."
  },
  {
   "h": "Cognitive — the hillbilly",
   "text": "The images we bring were made elsewhere: cartoons, reality TV, the Hatfields and McCoys, *Deliverance*. They make the region white, backward, violent and lazy. It is none of those as a rule. Black Appalachians (the Affrilachian poets named themselves) and Latino families are part of this place; a coal economy built roads, unions and hospitals; people here have researched and organized to change their own laws (Barrier 3)."
  },
  {
   "h": "Cognitive — the photograph",
   "text": "Since the 1960s, photographers have come to eastern Kentucky to take pictures of poverty — children on porches, broken cars, trash — that tell the outside world what it expected. People here call it poverty porn. **Do not photograph homes, clutter, homeowners or children without asking, and never post a before picture of someone's house.**"
  },
  {
   "h": "Affective",
   "text": "Pity, or its twin, they are so happy with so little.\" Awkwardness at accents, at churches that pray differently, at politics students assume they know. Fear of the stories about drugs. Keep three things apart: what you saw, what you were told and by whom, and what you concluded."
  },
  {
   "h": "Theological",
   "text": "We're so blessed — reading our comfort as God's favor. Treating Protestant mountain faith as less than ours, or Kentucky as a mission field for Catholics. The people here have been Christians in these hollows for two centuries; their churches were feeding flood survivors before we arrived."
  }
 ],
